<statement>
<p>
	Given a row vector of digits, this program returns the positive integer with these digits. This is just the reverse process of the previous problem. That is, given the digits of a number reconstruct the number.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>digit_list</pf> (1 x n) double vector — vector containing the digits of a number (integers).</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>num</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer with digits from <pf>digit_list</pf></p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>This is an easier problem than the previous one since you can use the fact that every integer has a ones digit, tens digit, hundredths digit, thousandths digit, etc.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>For example,</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>or</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>So to get the number you just need to compute the quantity on the right side of either expression above.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		INPUT
		[3, 0, 0, 4, 6, 1]
		OUTPUT
		300461
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
